            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Lycurgus of Sparta</strong><br>\nLycurgus is a legendary figure in the history of Sparta, known for establishing the city-state's military-oriented reformation. His life dates are uncertain, but he is believed to have lived in the 9th century BCE. As a lawgiver, his reforms were aimed at creating a disciplined and austere society that prioritized military prowess. He is credited with establishing the Spartan constitution, which included elements like the Gerousia (council of elders) and the Apella (assembly of citizens).</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Spartan Social Structure</strong><br>\nThe Spartan society was divided into three main classes: Spartiates, Perioeci, and Helots. Spartiates were full citizens who were professional soldiers. The Perioeci were free inhabitants who engaged in commerce and industry. The Helots were state-owned serfs who worked the lands owned by the Spartiates. Lycurgus's laws were designed to maintain this strict social hierarchy and ensure a powerful, efficient state.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Agoge System</strong><br>\nUnder Lycurgus's laws, Spartan education, known as the agoge, became compulsory and rigorous. Boys were enrolled at the age of seven into a system that focused on physical conditioning, survival skills, and loyalty to the state. This education lasted until they were 30 years old, preparing them for a life of military service and emphasizing communal living and strict discipline.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
