      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Lycurgus's Economic Reforms</strong><br>\nTo promote equality and focus on military efficiency, Lycurgus is said to have introduced significant economic reforms. He redistributed land equally among the citizens and introduced an iron currency to discourage hoarding and to make wealth accumulation difficult. This was intended to eliminate economic disparities and luxury, reinforcing the idea of communal living and shared values.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Syssitia: Communal Dining</strong><br>\nAnother central aspect of Lycurgus's reforms was the syssitia, or communal meals. All Spartan men were required to dine together in communal messes to strengthen bonds and ensure that no citizen lived in a manner that was either too luxurious or too impoverished. These meals were simple and frugal, further promoting the values of equality and austerity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Spartan Military Dominance</strong><br>\nLycurgus's reforms led to the establishment of one of the most formidable military powers in ancient Greece. The emphasis on military training from a young age resulted in highly disciplined and skilled warriors. The Spartans' reputation for valor and efficiency in battle was unmatched during their height, owing largely to the societal foundations laid by Lycurgus's legislation.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
