          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Role of Spartan Women</strong><br>\nIn contrast to other Greek city-states, Spartan women had more freedom and responsibility. They were expected to maintain physical fitness to bear strong children and to manage the household in the absence of their warrior husbands. Lycurgus's reforms allowed Spartan women to own land and participate in athletics, which was revolutionary for the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Gerousia and Apella</strong><br>\nThe Spartan constitution created by Lycurgus included the Gerousia, a council of elders that advised the kings, and the Apella, an assembly of male citizens who had the power to pass laws and make decisions. This political structure provided a balance of power and allowed for a form of checks and balances in the Spartan government, with elders providing wisdom and the people having a voice.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Lycurgus</strong><br>\nThe impact of Lycurgus's reforms extended beyond his lifetime, influencing the culture and society of Sparta for centuries. His laws promoted a strong sense of community, equality among Spartiates, and established a warrior society that was unique in ancient Greece. The exact details of his life and work remain shrouded in mystery, but his legacy as a lawgiver and the architect of the Spartan way of life endures in historical accounts.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
